2026-11-22 12:18:12 - Agent_20261122121527 - INFO - [+] Agent signaled completion: Reviewed the webbrowser.open/launch call-site group in Click's terminal UI implementation.

1. Query 20261122120430 (src/click/_termui_impl.py:737) — `webbrowser.open(url)` in the xdg-open fallback: classified **false positive**. The value is only reached after `subprocess.Popen(["xdg-open", url])` raises OSError, and it is guarded by `url.startswith(("http://", "https://")) and not locate and not wait`. `webbrowser.open` is not a shell sink (it uses webbrowser controllers, not a shell), and the http/https prefix constraint prevents local-file/`file://` and option injection. No untrusted-input-to-execution path exists; the URL is supplied by the developer via the public `click.launch()` API.

2. New finding — src/click/_termui_impl.py:599 (`Editor.edit_files`): `subprocess.Popen(args=f"{editor} {exc_filename}", shell=True)` builds a shell command line by naively joining filenames with quotes (no escaping). Filenames containing `"` , backticks or `$()` break out of the quoting and execute shell metacharacters; `editor` itself comes from `$VISUAL`/`$EDITOR`, allowing argument/command injection. Flagged **tp** (command injection via `click.edit(filename=...)`), with a lower-severity manual-review note recorded for the pager path at line 451 where `shell=True` is combined with an argv list built from pager command params, whose residual shell-injection surface depends on caller-controlled flags.

Reviewed supporting context: `click.launch()` at src/click/termui.py:776-802 (thin wrapper passing URL straight through), `_unquote_file` handling of `file://` URLs, and the Windows/Cygwin subprocess branches which use argv lists and are therefore not shell-injectable.
